\section{Mathematical Tools}\label{subsec:math-tools}
The following theorem is used multiple times through the runtime analysis of the \MMAHH to upper bound the expectation of a sum of random variables whose range also depends on a random variable.

\begin{theorem}[Simplified version of Wald’s equation -- \cite{Wald44}; \cite{DoerrK15}]\label{thm:wald}
    Let $T$ be a random variable with bounded expectation and let $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be a sequence of non-negative random variables. If there exists a constant $C > 0$ such that for any $i \geq 0$ we have $\esp[ X_i \mid T \geq i] \leq C$ then
        \[ \esp\left[ \sum_{i = 1}^T X_i \right] \leq C \esp[T]. \]
\end{theorem}

We now state two drifts theorems, one is a more general version of the standard additive drift theorem and allows \emph{overshooting}, i.e.,  the sequence of random variables can exceed the target but at the price of an additional terms, the expected overshooting, in the upper bound. The other one is the multiplicative drift theorem which provides an upper bound when the expected progress linearly depends on the distance to the target.

