\begin{abstract}
Empirical evaluation of state-of-the-art natural-language (NL) to temporal-logic (TL) translation systems reveals near-perfect performance on existing benchmarks. However, current studies measure only the accuracy of the \emph{translation} of NL logic into formal TL, ignoring a system’s capacity to \emph{ground} atomic propositions into new scenarios or environments. This is a critical feature, necessary for the \emph{verification} of resulting formulas in a concrete state space. Consequently, most NL-to-TL translation frameworks propose their own bespoke dataset in which the correct grounding is known a-priori, inflating performance metrics and neglecting the need for extensible, domain‑general systems. In this paper, we introduce \textbf{\fullname{} (\name)}, a unifying benchmark that measures verification and verifiability of automated NL-to-LTL translation. 
The dataset consists of three unique state spaces and thousands of diverse natural language specifications and corresponding formal specifications in temporal logic. Moreover, the benchmark contains sample traces to validate the temporal logic expressions. While the benchmark directly supports end-to-end evaluation, we observe that many frameworks decompose the process into i) lifting,  ii) grounding, iii) translation, and iv) verification. The benchmark provides ground truths after each of these steps to enable researches to improve and evaluate different substeps of the overall problem.  
 Using the benchmark, we evaluate several state‑of‑the‑art NL-to-TL translation models and frameworks, including nl2spec, NL2TL, NL2LTL, sequence-to-sequence translation, and various prompting techniques. 
 Our evaluation reveals that existing work is capable of reliably performing lifting and translation with high accuracy but struggle with grounding the translation in a state space, which stems from the lack of datasets to drive the research. Moreover, we provide traces that can be used for integrating verification into translation process, unlocking further directions for future research. To encourage  methodologically sound advances in verifiable NL-to-LTL translation approaches, we release \name{} here: \url{https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/dubascudes/vltl-bench}.

 %. We provide \hl{three} baselines for the grounding using VLMs and LLMs and observe that further research efforts are needed. 


 %Our evaluation reveals the hidden cost of verification: lifted translation relies on successfully grounding into new scenarios. Even after domain‑specific lifting, accuracy on unseen environments remains below \hl{\mbox{C\%}}, highlighting the need for generalizable solutions. By introducing grounded datasets with sample traces included alongside NL-LTL pairs, \name{} provides the first rigorous yardstick for practical NL-to-LTL translation. We release all code, data, and evaluation scripts to spur methodologically sound advances in formal‑language grounding at scale.
\end{abstract}






\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Formal verification is essential for the safe deployment of autonomous robots \cite{RobotLanguage, Spec2}, cyber–physical controllers \cite{SurveyTemporal}, and safety‑critical software systems \cite{SAS_textbook_PoCPS}. Verification first begins with a specification that defines intent in precise temporal‑logic (TL) \cite{watson2005autonomous, bellini2000temporal}. However, human stakeholders typically articulate intent in ambiguous natural language (NL) \cite{veizaga2021systematically, lamar2009linguistic, lafi2021eliciting} and the conversion of this NL to TL is a challenging and time-consuming process that requires human experts \citep{yin2024formal, cardoso2021review, thistle1986control}. Due to this complexity, automated translation of NL to TL has emerged as a core research problem \cite{NL2TL, Natural2CTL_data, circuits_data, conformalLTL_data}. Recently, neural seq‑to‑seq models \cite{hahn2022formal, pan2023data, hsiung2022generalizing}, grammar‑constrained decoders \cite{post2018fastlexicallyconstraineddecoding, geng2024grammarconstraineddecodingstructurednlp}, and large language models (LLMs) \cite{xu2024learning, NL2TL, NL2LTL, nl2spec} have all demonstrated promising results on benchmark corpora, with reported string‑exact accuracies often exceeding $90\%$. Despite these gains, we find existing evaluations provide an incomplete — and sometimes misleading — picture of true semantic competence. Specifically, we observe that high translation accuracies typically report the correctness of \emph{lifted} (Section \ref{prelim:lifting}) TL predictions. While these expressions are syntactically correct, and indeed are equivalent to the expected \emph{lifted} TL, there is little investigation into a framework's ability to \emph{ground} the lifted atomic propositions into a world state space. This oversight can be attributed to the widespread lack of an accompanying concrete world state space in existing corpora. 

