Current approaches for automated NL-to-TL translation are characterized by the use of multiple components, each with a distinct purpose in the end-to-end translation process. As noted earlier, we categorize these components into either lifting, grounding, or translation. Many frameworks employ different combinations of these components, such as nl2spec \cite{nl2spec}, which presupposes a \emph{pre-lifted and pre-grounded} natural language specification as input. For example, an input "Globally, grant 0 and grant 1 do not hold at the same time until it is allowed," is assumed to be accompanied by a dictionary that maps the natural language sub-string "grant 1" to a known atomic proposition $g_1$. Therefore, this framework lacks the components for lifting and grounding, but includes a human auditing feature, which we omit from our analysis of automated translation frameworks.  Meanwhile, another SOTA approach NL2TL \cite{NL2TL} is divided into two processes. First, an LLM - in this case GPT3.5 \citep{achiam2023gpt} - is prompted to extract the atomic propositions (APs) from the NL input, creating ``lifted NL" (NL without complex propositions). This new, less complex representation is then translated by a trained sequence-to-sequence model, such as T5 \citep{raffel2020exploring}. For example, an NL specification for a system: ``You have an apple until you eat it.", will be converted into the lifted NL: ``\texttt{prop1} until \texttt{prop2}.". Here, \texttt{prop1} is ``You have an apple" and \texttt{prop2} is ``you eat it". This lifted NL is then translated to the lifted TL: ``\texttt{prop1} $\cup$ \texttt{prop2}." and the lifted propositions would be replaced. 

These two frameworks are representative of the general research effort to produce better NL-to-TL translation frameworks, and yet no current frameworks have a holistic and faithful approach to translation. Therefore, we note the need for a benchmark that includes the data required to evaluate all components of the end-to-end translation pipeline - lifting, grounding, translation, and verification - in order to provide a complete picture of a framework's true performance. We compare existing datasets and benchmarks on the basis of the linguistic and logical complexity of the end-to-end input and output, as well as the inclusion of features required to measure the performance of each individual component in Table \ref{tab:qual_data_comp}.

We address these gaps with the \textbf{\fullname{} (\name{})}, a benchmark that grounds linear temporal logic (LTL) in a concrete world state space while broadening linguistic and logical coverage. \name{} introduces three new datasets which contain both raw and lifted versions of NL specifications, an AP-to-Condition dictionary, lifted and grounded LTL, and examples of satisfying and unsatisfying traces. The provision of each of these elements enables us to evaluate the four abstract components present in emerging NL-to-TL translation frameworks. Furthermore, the inclusion of example traces allows us to further evaluate the semantic consistency of translation and grounding attempts, resulting in a new \emph{verification accuracy} performance metric, which measures whether an output translation holds on an a given trace. This feature permits consistency checking against real formal traces- a heretofore omitted feature of NL-to-TL translation corpora which will improve future explorations of NL-to-TL translation. In plausible real-world applications, a system may be given some example traces with which to grade its own translation attempts. This provides the opportunity to improve translation attempts \textit{without} human auditing. We believe that the ability to measure the performance of individual components of a given framework will clarify the weaknesses of existing frameworks and guide future work. We outline our dataset synthesis and translation evaluation pipelines in Figure \ref{fig:overview}.

In summary, our contributions are: (i) a single, extensible benchmark and data synthesis pipeline for the evaluation of all current NL-to-TL translation components; (ii) a first-of-its-kind evaluation of the verification potential of existing frameworks, using our example satisfying and unsatisfying traces; and (iii) an empirical study that exposes both new failure modes in current translation methods and the severe accuracy decline when grounding is required. By providing a rigorous and extensible yardstick, \name{} can catalyze methodologically sound advances in NL-to-TL translation and, more broadly, in grounding large language models for formal reasoning tasks.


