\subsection{Benchmark Datasets}

 The \textbf{Cleanup World (CW)} dataset \citet{CW_data} pairs NL commands with reward functions in an OO‑MDP. While this work introduces a supervised grounding approach, the dataset used for training and evaluation is limited in terms of logical and linguistic complexity. This is due to the minimal set of APs used to construct the examples, and lack of diversity in both AP representation and sentence structure. Similarly, \textbf{GLTL} \citet{GLTL-data} gathers English sentences mapped to \emph{Geometric LTL} formulas in robot‑navigation scenarios, emphasizing stochastic‑window operators but remaining modest in scale. The contents are remarkably similar to Cleanup World in both quality and metrics, with both datasets having the same number of unique temporal logic phrases and unique APs. The use of LLMs to enhance sentence diversity had not yet been adopted in the field at this time, and no alternate AP representations are introduced in the natural language. Therefore, the evaluation abilities of the GLTL dataset are limited in largely the same manner as Cleanup World. The \textbf{Navi.} corpus, introduced by \citet{Navi_data} couples NL commands with LTL formulas and execution traces in a grid world. Its 226 unique APs make it a strong test of translation and lexical robustness, though this improvement comes at the cost of grounding- the Navi corpus does not specify formal APs, providing instead POS-tagged natural language representations, as shown below. Most recently, the \textbf{ConformalNL2LTL} dataset, introduced by \citet{conformalLTL_data}, frames translation as open‑vocabulary QA wrapped in conformal prediction (CP) to provide statistical guarantees. If the cumulative CP $p$‑value falls below a user‑specified threshold, the system escalates to a human; otherwise, it commits the subformula. The authors release a 1000‑example corpus of grounded robot tasks partitioned by difficulty. We provide examples and a quantitative evaluation of existing datasets in Appendix \ref{appendix:existing_data_examples}.


\section{\fullname{}}
\label{sec:method}

Our framework is built around two core stages: \emph{Grounded Scenario Configuration}, which defines the domain in which every proposition lives, and \emph{Data Synthesis}, which instantiates our library of expert templates with scenario‐specific atoms and then prunes any invalid NL. In the first stage, we provide the canonical formalization of the agent and the world it operates in. In the second stage, we apply the canonical APs obtained from the first to our expert-defined NL, TL, and trace templates.

\subsection{Grounded Scenario Configuration}
\label{sec:scenarios}

Each dataset is parameterized by a \emph{scenario}—a small, declarative world model that provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Targets} $\mathcal{L}$: e.g.\ \{\texttt{location}, \texttt{item}\} or \{\texttt{street}, \texttt{light}\}.
  \item \textbf{Actions} $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{args}}$: verbs the agent may perform (e.g.\ \texttt{idle}, \texttt{deliver(item, location)}, \texttt{search(item)}).
  \item \textbf{Target types} $t\in\mathcal{T}$: each with its own action set $\mathcal{A}_t$ and argument signatures (e.g.\ \texttt{deliver(item,location)}, \texttt{avoid(threat)}).
\end{itemize}
By swapping the scenario description of $\mathcal{L}$, $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{args}}$, and $\{\mathcal{A}_t\}$, the same logical and linguistic templates can be deployed in multiple domains without changing the core synthesis code.

\subsection{Data Synthesis}
\label{sec:synthesis}

% To produce our datasets, we examined the catalog of 36 expert-crafted lifted NL-LTL pairs introduced by \citet{nl2spec} as part of the nl2spec benchmark combined with 7 of our own, and manufacture two additional components corresponding to each of them- satisfied and unsatisfied traces. In this manner, we transform a small set of lifted examples into a set of templates that can be used for diverse NL-LTL synthesis. In total, each template consists of:
% \begin{enumerate}
%   \item An LTL skeleton (e.g.$\texttt{globally prop\_1 implies prop\_2 })$),
%   \item A matching natural‐language pattern, lifted such that action and target references from our scenarios are combined to form NL APs,
%   \item Two corresponding \emph{trace template} that yields a minimal satisfying ($\sigma_{good} \models \varphi_G$) and violating ($\,\sigma_{bad}\not\models \varphi_G)$) trace.
% \end{enumerate}
To produce our datasets, we started with the 36 expert-crafted lifted NL-LTL pairs introduced by \citet{nl2spec} in the nl2spec benchmark and we crafted 7 of our own. We then crafted 43 pairs of corresponding example traces to each NL-LTL pair. Following this, we transform these lifted examples into a set of templates that can be used for diverse NL-LTL synthesis, introducing three substantial increases in complexity. 
\begin{enumerate}
  \item We generate more diverse words for objects, actions, and items as a potential scenario to enhance each example. 
  \item Each LTL skeleton (e.g. $\texttt{globally(prop\_1 implies prop\_2)}$) is coupled to action and target references from these scenarios.
  \item Each example is given a corresponding pair of \emph{trace templates} that yield a minimal satisfying ($\sigma_{good} \models \varphi_G$) and violating ($\,\sigma_{bad}\not\models \varphi_G$) trace.
\end{enumerate}

To generate one example:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Atom sampling.}  Draw $k$ unique atomic propositions by randomly selecting actions and arguments from the scenario’s $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ego}}\cup\mathcal{A}_t$ and $\mathcal{L}$.
  \item \textbf{Template instantiation.}  Uniformly choose a lifted template whose arity $\le k$.  Fill its LTL skeleton with the $k$ canonical atoms to obtain the \emph{grounded} formula $\varphi_g$, and replace each atom by $\mathsf{prop}_i$ to obtain the \emph{lifted} formula $\varphi_L$.
  \item \textbf{NL realization.}  Fill the semantic pattern with each atom’s surface‐form (including articles/prepositions), apply morphological fixes (gerunds, capitalization), and record token‐level spans.  Emit both the free‐form sentence and its \texttt{grounded\_sentence} with explicit $\mathsf{prop}_i$ placeholders.
  \item \textbf{Trace filling.}  Apply the template’s trace builder to the list $[\mathsf{prop}_1,\dots,\mathsf{prop}_k]$, yielding one \emph{good} trace (satisfies $\varphi_G$) and one \emph{bad} trace (violates $\varphi_G$).
  \item \textbf{Pruning.}  Submit each candidate NL sentence to GPT-4o for a lightweight check of the natural language components, removing any entries that the model flags as nonsensical or incorrect from the benchmark set.
\end{enumerate}



\subsection{Metrics}
Each dataset entry includes three paired artifacts:
\[
  \bigl\{\,
    \underbrace{\mathrm{sentence},\,\mathrm{lifted \ sentence\}}}_{\text{NL (grounded \& lifted)}},\;
    \underbrace{\varphi_G,\,\varphi_L}_{\text{LTL (grounded \& lifted)}},\;
    \underbrace{\sigma_{good} \models \varphi_G,\,\sigma_{bad}\not\models \varphi_G}_{\text{Traces (holds \& $\neg$ holds)}}
  \bigr\}.
\]
This rich annotation supports four independent evaluation axes, displayed in Figure \ref{fig:metrix}:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Lifting accuracy:} The ability to correctly extract substrings from a natural language specification that map to a corresponding atomic proposition in the linear temporal logic. 
  \item \textbf{Translation accuracy:} The fidelity of mapping grounded \textit{or} lifted NL to the correct temporal‐logic formula.
    \item \textbf{Grounding accuracy:} The ability to align logical atomic proposition labels with their corresponding world state condition.
  \item \textbf{Verification accuracy:} The capacity to decide satisfaction or violation of a grounded LTL formula on a given trace or traces.
\end{enumerate}

