\paragraph{Warehouse.}
Our \emph{Warehouse} dataset simulates a realistic warehouse retrieval scenario, explicitly designed for scalability and complexity in grounding tasks. Warehouse is our most distinct dataset with its inclusion of all 80 COCO \cite{lin2014microsoft} object classes, significantly enriching the semantic and linguistic complexity and variation of atomic propositions (APs). Each entry includes LTL formulas with explicit grounding and alignment at token-level granularity. The dataset also uniquely provides verified positive ("good") and negative ("bad") execution traces for robust validation. 

\textbf{Example:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sentence:} ``At every moment, at least one of drop off the long chair to the loading dock, wait, or look for the glass for alcoholic beverage holds.'' 
    \item \textbf{Lifted Sentence:} ``At every moment, at least one of prop\_1, prop\_2 or prop\_3 holds.''
    \item \textbf{Grounded LTL Formula:} globally( deliver(bench, loading\_dock) or idle or search(wine\_glass))
    \item \textbf{APs:} prop\_1 = ``drop off long chair to loading dock'', prop\_2 = ``idle'', prop\_3 = ``look for glass for alcoholic beverage''
    % \item Good Trace: [deliver(bench,loading\_dock)], [idle], [search(wine\_glass)]
    % \item Bad Trace: []
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Search and Rescue.}
The \emph{Search-and-Rescue} (S\&R) scenario presents a detailed context of emergency response and robotic aid. It emphasizes multi-step TL relationships, requiring precise event sequencing and robust conditional propositions. Like the Warehouse scenario, it provides complete grounding information and includes scenario-specific LTL templates with both positive and negative execution traces for rigorous verification. The S\&R scenario deliberately includes an "avoid" action for the purpose of stress-testing the a framework's ability to distinguish between APs and LTL operators, because avoid is commonly used to indicate a "globally (negation $\varphi$)" pattern.

\textbf{Example:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sentence:} ``If deliver aid to the safe rescuer holds and request assistance holds next, then stay away from the active gas leak holds in the step after that.'' 
    \item \textbf{Lifted Sentence:} ``If prop\_1 holds and prop\_2 holds next, then prop\_3 holds in the step after that.''
    \item \textbf{Grounded LTL Formula:} globally(deliver\_aid(safe\_rescuer) and next get\_help implies next next avoid(active\_gas\_leak))
    \item \textbf{APs:} prop\_1 = deliver aid to safe rescuer, prop\_2 = request assistance, prop\_3 = avoid active gas leak
    % \item Good Trace: [deliver\_aid(safe\_rescuer)], [get\_help], [avoid(active\_gas\_leak)]
    % \item Bad Trace: [deliver\_aid(safe\_rescuer)], [get\_help], []
\end{itemize}



\paragraph{Traffic Light Control.}
The \emph{Traffic Light Control} scenario introduces LTL propositions relevant to urban navigation and infrastructure management. This scenario tests an NL-to-LTL system’s ability to accurately ground complex real-world relationships, such as spatial landmarks and persistent behaviors like "infinitely often." Each dataset entry is provided with explicit good and bad traces, detailed proposition grounding, and is constructed via a flexible scenario template, allowing straightforward extension to additional real-world tasks.

\textbf{Example:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Sentence:} ``Take a photo of the motorcycle on northeast 10th avenue must keep holding until, from some point on, change east light red holds infinitely often.'' 
    \item \textbf{Lifted Sentence: }``prop\_1 must keep holding until prop\_2 holds infinitely often.''
    \item \textbf{Grounded LTL Formula:} photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue) until globally(finally change(light\_east,red))
    \item \textbf{APs:} prop\_1 = take a photo of motorcycle, northeast 10th avenue, prop\_2 = change east light red
    % \item Good Trace: [photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue)], [photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue)], [change(light\_east,red)]
    % \item Bad Trace: [photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue)], [photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue)], [photo(motorcycle,northeast\_10th\_avenue)]
\end{itemize}


\section{Experimental Results}
\label{sec:experiments}
In this section, we present the results of multiple evaluations of NL-to-LTL translation frameworks and components. In Section \ref{subsec:lifting_eval}, we measure the performance of common natural language lifting approaches, evaluated on four existing datasets in addition to the three datasets we present in \name{}. In Section \ref{subsec:translation_eval}, we evaluate three SOTA NL-to-LTL frameworks on lifted NL to lifted TL translation. Note here, that measuring lifted translation performance on existing datasets is particularly difficult, as they present varying degrees of clarity in their lifted natural language elements. The CW \cite{CW_data}, GLTL \cite{GLTL-data}, and Navi \cite{Navi_data} datasets have been processed to include lifted natural language components by \citet{NL2TL} - and we perform similar processing of the Conformal dataset \cite{conformalLTL_data} to include it in our evaluation. In Section \ref{subsec:grounding_eval} we develop and evaluate two grounding baselines on our three datasets. In Section \ref{subsec:endtoend_eval}, we assemble the best results from the three individual evaluations to perform the first end-to-end translation evaluation. In Section \ref{subsec:verification_eval} we perform our novel verification evaluation over the example traces of our dataset. 


