\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Given various machine learning (ML) applications in the real world, concerns about discrimination and unfairness hidden in ML models are growing, particularly in high-stakes domains \citep{sanchez2020does,mhasawade2021machine,rodolfa2021empirical,chen2023algorithmic,jones2024causal}. 
Great attention has therefore been paid to bias mitigation and fairness enhancement in ML models, such as pre-processing \citep{kamiran2012data,zemel2013learning,feldman2015certifying,calmon2017optimized,chierichetti2017fair,kazemi2018scalable,amadi2018price,du2018data,sharma2020data,wang2022improving}, in-processing \citep{kamiran2010discrimination,kamishima2012fairness,vapnik2015learning,zafar2017fairness1,zafar2017fairness2,woodworth2017learning,bechavod2017penalizing,quadrianto2017recycling,berk2017convex,agarwal2018reductions,agarwal2019fair,zehlike2020reducing,zhang2021farf,serna2022sensitive}, and post-processing \citep{hardt2016equality,corbett2017algorithmic,menon2018cost,dwork2018decoupled,kamiran2018exploiting,lohia2019bias} mechanisms, depending on which duration manipulations are applied in training. 

In the technologies of building fair and trustworthy ML models, detecting discrimination is critical because even relatively small social and political biases that go undetected can lead to adverse consequences, for example through human-algorithmic feedback loops \citep{hu2024generative,glickman2024human}. 
Specifically, the tendency of ML systems to amplify biases would be further internalised through human-AI interactions, which is much greater than that observed in interactions between humans, triggering a snowball effect in which small errors in judgment escalate into larger or worse errors \citep{glickman2024human}. 
Therefore, it is quite essential to develop well-defined fairness measures to properly and correctly address discrimination mitigation problems in ML systems. 

However, the existing fairness measures may fall short of mitigating discrimination effectively for two reasons. 
First, they are often incompatible with each other \citep{barocas2019fairness,barocas2023fairness}, meaning that unfairness may still exist even when some fairness measures are satisfied. 
Second, they are limited to narrow scenarios, such as one single bi-valued sensitive attribute (SA). 
Few of them can deal with scenarios where more than one SA with multiple potential values exist. 
More importantly, we observe some opinions in the community---especially, outside the fairness researcher community---through verbal discussions and peer-review procedures that: 1) Instead of proposing new computational methods for a fairness quantity, it would be more valuable to provide a tool to assist researchers in making decisions concerning their ML pipeline on the basis of a fairness quantity; 
2) The mathematical correctness in the design of fairness measures is far more important than societal considerations, where the latter is not the job of computer scientists to consider. 

We strongly disagree with such views. 
Although we acknowledge the value of providing tools to assist researchers in making decisions in their ML pipelines based on some fairness quantity, the premise of using one fairness quantity is that we have one fairness quantity to use. 
Besides, what if the used fairness quantity in ML pipelines is too rough to capture enough information about unfairness, wouldn't it be counterproductive when the ML pipeline was guided by some less proper or suitable fairness quantity in use? 
For instance, binarizing multi-valued SAs may not capture discrimination levels precisely, and merging multiple SAs into one can increase processing time. 
Thus, our first position in this paper is that \emph{the importance of properly defined fairness measures is much underestimated}. 
In other words, \textbf{\em developing fairness measures that effectively handle single multi-valued or multiple sensitive attributes is essential and urgently needed to address growing discrimination concerns in ML systems}. 
Furthermore, our second position here is that \textbf{\em societal considerations need to be integrated into fairness ML research}, because the concept of fairness is clearly relevant to social sciences and even more (such as law, politics, economics, and philosophy). 
Frothy mathematical equations and their self-consistent logic cannot directly become the perfect answer and fully solve real-world problems if their societal meanings are ignored. 


In the following, we will start with summarising existing fairness definitions/measures in Section~\ref{sec:relat}, in order to support our first position. Then we argue with several alternative views that we observed from verbal discussions and reviewers from a wide computer science community in Section~\ref{sec:alter}, including why we still need more refined fairness measures, and in which both positions of ours are explained. 
At last, we conclude and restate our positions with Section~\ref{sec:concl}. 


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:relat}




In this section, we summarise existing definitions/measures of fairness, starting with a list of some standard notations that we use throughout this paper. 
Then we separately introduce distributive fairness (centered around the outcomes/predictions of the decision process) in Sections~\ref{subsec:lit1} to \ref{subsec:lit3} and procedural fairness in Section~\ref{subsec:lit4}, where the latter is based on the decision-making process rather than the outcomes. 
We list a few worth-noting remarks in Section~\ref{subsec:lit5}.






\subsection{Preliminaries}
\label{subsec:lit0}
In this paper, we denote
\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{-.4mm}%
\item scalars by italic lowercase letters (\eg{} $x$),
\item vectors by bold lowercase letters (\eg{} $\mathbf{x}$),
\item matrices/sets by italic uppercase letters (\eg{} $X$),
\item random variables by serif uppercase letters (\eg{} $\mathsf{X}$),
\item real numbers (resp. integers, and positive integers) by $\mathbb{R}$ (resp. $\mathbb{Z}$, and $\mathbb{Z}_+$),
\item probability measure (resp. expectation, and variance of one random variable) by $\mathbb{P}(\cdot)$ (resp. $\mathbb{E}(\cdot)$, and $\mathbb{V}(\cdot)$),
\item hypothesis space (resp. models or predictors or learners or classifiers in this space) by $\mathcal{F}$ (resp. $f(\cdot)$).
\end{itemize}


Here, for one unknown distribution $\probD$ over $\mathcal{X\times A\times Y}= \mathbb{R}^{n_d}\times \mathbb{Z}^{n_a}\times \{0,1,...,n_c-1\}$ where $\mathcal{X\times A}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$ are, respectively, the feature/input space and label/output space. 
Note that $n_a, n_d, n_c\in\mathbb{Z}_+$ and they are the numbers of sensitive/protected attributes, remaining/insensitive/unprotected attributes, and labels, respectively. 
In general, vanilla ML tasks aim to optimise the expected predictive accuracy, that is, minimising the expected accuracy risk 
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
    \err(f)= 1-\acc(f)=
    \mathbb{E}_{(\xneg,\xpos,y)\sim \probD}[
        \mathbb{I}( f(\xneg,\xpos) \neq y )
    ] \,.\label{eq:acc}
\end{equation}





The tasks relevant to discrimination/bias mitigation are usually binary (that is, $\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}$ or $\{+1,-1\}$, and $n_c=2$); we will explicitly indicate it if the analysis also applies to multi-class classification (that is, $n_c\geqslant 3$). 
In these tasks, usually a predictor function $f:\mathcal{X\times\mathcal{A}} \mapsto\mathcal{Y}$ is expected to be learned. 
A scoring function $h: \mathcal{X\times\mathcal{A}} \mapsto[0,1]$ may be introduced in some literature, and in that case, 
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    f(\xneg,\xpos)=1 \text{ \iff{} } 
    h(\xneg,\xpos) \geqslant \text{threshold}
    \,.\label{eq:fxh}
\end{equation}

It is worth noting that most existing fairness measures in the literature only work for one single SA (that is, $n_a=1$, and $\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_1\subseteq\mathbb{Z}$ is a finite set), usually with binary values (that is, $\mathcal{A}_1=\{0,1\}$ or $\{1,2\}$); we will explicitly indicate it if the corresponding assessment also applies to multiple SAs (that is, $n_a\geqslant 2$). 
Throughout this paper, we keep using $a_1=1$ to represent the corresponding instance belonging to the privileged group and $a_1\neq 1$ that belonging to marginalised group(s), which is suitable for both $n_{a_1}\defineq |\mathcal{A}_1|=2$ and $n_{a_1}>2$ where $\mathcal{A}_1=\{1,2,...,n_{a_1}\}$. 



Note that, in the following, we may abbreviate $\mathbb{E}_{(\xneg,\xpos,y)\sim \probD}[\cdot]$ and $\mathbb{P}_{(\xneg,\xpos,y)\sim \probD}(\cdot)$ as $\probE[\cdot]$ and $\probP(\cdot)$, respectively, for brevity when the context is unambiguous. 
We also use $i\in[n]$ to represent $i\in\{1,2,...,n\}$ for brevity. 











\subsection{Group fairness}
\label{subsec:lit1}

\begin{definition}[Demographic parity \citep{gajane2017formalizing,jiang2020wasserstein}, \aka{} statistical parity \citep{dwork2012fairness,chouldechova2017fair}]
\label{def:grp1,dp}%
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{DP}(f) =
    |&\hspace{1.2em} \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)=1 \mid a_1=0 ) \\
    &- \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)=1 \mid a_1=1 )
    | \leqslant\varepsilon
    \,.\label{eq:grp,dp}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Disparate impact (\ie{} ``80\% rule'') \citep{feldman2015certifying,zafar2017fairness2}]
\label{def:grp1,disi}%
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
    \mathrm{DisI}(f) =
    \frac{ \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!0 )
    }{ \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!1 )
    } \geqslant \tau=0.8 
    \,.\label{eq:grp,di}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Disparate treatment \citep{zafar2017fairness2,haas2019price}]
\label{def:grp1,dist}%
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{DisT}(f) =
    | \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1 )
    - \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 )
    |\,,\\ a_1\in\{0,1\}
    \,.\label{eq:grp,dt}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
It is also indicated as ``statistical parity'' in \citep{corbett2017algorithmic,agarwal2019fair}, that is,
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \prival[ 
    | \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!j )
    - \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 ) |]
    \,,\label{eq:sub,sp}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\mathsf{A}_1\in\mathcal{A}_1=\{1,2,...,n_{a_1}\}$. 
\end{definition}


\begin{definition}[Equalised odds \citep{hardt2016equality}]
\label{def:grp2,eodd}
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{EOdd}(f) =
    |&\!\! \hspace{1.2em} \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!0 ,\,y ) \\
    &\!\!- \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!1, y )
    |,\, y\in\{0,1\}
    \,.\label{eq:grp,eo'}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equality of opportunity \citep{hardt2016equality,gajane2017formalizing}, \aka{} treatment equality \citep{berk2021fairness}]
\label{def:grp2,eo}
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{EO}(f) =
    |&\hspace{1.2em} \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)=1 \mid a_1=0,\, y=1 ) \\
    &- \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)=1 \mid a_1=1,\, y=1 )
    | \leqslant\varepsilon
    \,.\label{eq:grp,eo}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}


% Predictive quality parity 
\begin{definition}[Predictive parity \citep{chouldechova2017fair,verma2018fairness}]
\label{def:grp3,pqp}
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{PP}(f) =
    |&\hspace{1.2em} \probP( y=1\mid a_1=0,\, f(\xneg,a_1)=1 ) \\
    &- \probP( y=1\mid a_1=1,\, f(\xneg,a_1)=1 )
    | \leqslant\varepsilon
    \,.\label{eq:grp,pp}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}



\begin{definition}[Conditional statistical parity \citep{corbett2017algorithmic}]
\label{def:grp1,csp}
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{CSP}(f) \!= \prival[ 
    |&\hspace{1em} \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid \ell(\xneg,a_1) ,\, a_1\!=\!j )
    \\&\!- \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid \ell(\xneg,a_1) )]
    \,,\label{eq:sub,cs}\nonumber
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\ell: \mathbb{R}^{n_d+1} \mapsto \mathbb{R}^{m}$ is a projection of features $(\xneg,a_1)$ to the factors that are considered ``legitimate''. 
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Predictive equality \citep{corbett2017algorithmic}]
\label{def:grp2,peq}
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    \mathrm{PEq}(f)= \prival[ 
    |&\hspace{1em} \probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid a_1\!=\!j,\, y\!=\!0 ) \\
    &\!-\probP( f(\xneg,a_1)\!=\!1 \mid y\!=\!0 ) |]
    \,.\label{eq:sub,pe}\nonumber
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}












\subsection{Individual fairness}
\label{subsec:lit2}




\begin{definition}[Lipschitz condition \citep{dwork2012fairness}]
\label{def:indv,lips}
A mapping/predictor $h\!: \mathcal{X\times A}_1 =\mathcal{X\times}\{0,1\} \mapsto[0,1]$ satisfies the $\lambda$-Lipschitz property if for any $(\xneg,a_1), (\xneg',a_1')$, 
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \dist_y( h(\xneg,a_1), h(\xneg',a_1') ) 
    \leqslant \lambda\cdot 
    \dist_x( (\xneg,a_1), (\xneg',a_1') )
    \,,\label{eq:ind,lip}
\end{equation}
where $\dist_y$ and $\dist_x$ are (task-specific) distance metrics. 


It can also be written as the probability Lipschitzness, \ie{}
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \probP\left(\frac{
        \dist_y( h(\xneg,a_1), h(\xneg',a_1') )
    }{
        \dist_x( (\xneg,a_1), (\xneg',a_1') )
    } \geqslant\epsilon
    \right) \leqslant\delta
    \,;\label{eq:ind,l1}\nonumber
\end{equation}
or the $(\epsilon\!-\!\delta)$ language formulation,
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \dist_x( (\xneg,a_1), (\xneg',a_1') )
    \!\leqslant\!\epsilon \Rightarrow
    \dist_y( h(\xneg,a_1), h(\xneg',a_1') )
    \!\leqslant\!\delta 
    \,,\nonumber\label{eq:ind,l2}
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon\geqslant 0$ and $\delta\geqslant 0$. 
Note that $\lambda$ is a positive constant.





Additionally, in \citet{gajane2017formalizing}, 
a predictor satisfies individual fairness if and only if: $h(\xneg,a_1) \approx h(\xneg',a_1') \mid \dist_x((\xneg,a_1),(\xneg',a_1')) \approx 0$, where $\mathcal{X}_a\defineq \mathcal{X\!\times\! A}$ and $\dist_x: \mathcal{X}_a \times \mathcal{X}_a \mapsto\mathbb{R}$ is a distance metric for individuals. 


In essence, individual fairness follows the principle that ``similar individuals should be evaluated or treated similarly.'' 
A careful choice of distance metrics is crucial in ensuring fairness \citep{luong2011k,boeschoten2021achieving}. 
\end{definition}




\begin{definition}[General entropy indices \citep{speicher2018unified} and the Theil index \citep{haas2019price}]
\label{def:indv,gei}
For a constant $\alpha\notin\{0,1\}$, the generalised entropy indices for a problem with $n$ instances are defined, to quantify algorithmic unfairness, as
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \mathrm{GEI}^\alpha = \textstyle
    \frac{1}{n\alpha(\alpha-1)}
    \sum_{i=1}^n \left(
        \left(\frac{b_i}{\mu}\right)^\alpha
    -1 \right) \,,
\end{equation}
where benefits $b_i= f(\xneg_i,a_{1i})-y_i+1$ and $\mu=\sfrac{\sum_i b_i}{n}$. 


The Theil index is a special case for $\alpha=1$, that is, 
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \mathrm{Theil}= \textstyle
    \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n
    \frac{b_i}{\mu} \log
    \left(\frac{b_i}{\mu}\right)
    \,.
\end{equation}
They are used additionally to group fairness measures to compare different algorithms and determine which one is considered the fairest from an individual perspective. 
\end{definition}





\begin{definition}[Counterfactual fairness (CFF) \citep{kusner2017counterfactual,gajane2017formalizing}]
\label{def:causal,cff}
Given a causal model\footnote{%
%
A causal model is defined as a triple $(\cffU,\cffV,\cffF)$ where: 
1) $\cffU$ is a set of latent background variables, unaffected by any observable variable in $\cffV$; 
2) $\cffF$ is a set of structural equations $\{f_1,...,f_m\}$, each defining a variable $\cffIV\in\cffV$ as $\cffIV=f_i(\cffPA,\mathsf{U}_{\cffPA})$, where $\cffPA\subseteq \cffV\setminus\{\cffIV\}$ (the `parents' of $\cffIV$) and $\mathsf{U}_{\cffPA}\subseteq \cffU$. 
The model is causal because, given a distribution $\mathsf{P}(\cffU)$ over the background variables $\cffU$, the distribution of any subset $\cffZ\subseteq \cffV$ can be derived after an intervention on $\cffV\setminus \cffZ$. 
An intervention on $\cffIV$ is the substitution of $\cffIV= f_i(\cffPA,\mathsf{U}_{\cffPA})$ with $\cffIV=v$ for some $v$. 
%
} $(\cffU,\cffV,\cffF)$ where $\cffV\equiv \mathsf{A\cup X}$, a predictor $f(\cdot)$ is \emph{counterfactual fair} if under any context $\mathsf{X}=\xneg$ and $\mathsf{A}=\xpos$,
\begin{equation}
\topequation\small
\begin{split}
    & \probP( f_{\mathsf{A}\gets \xpos}(\mathsf{U})=y \mid \mathsf{X}=\xneg,\, \mathsf{A}=\xpos )\\=
    & \probP( f_{\mathsf{A}\gets \xpos'}(\mathsf{U})=y \mid \mathsf{X}=\xneg,\mathsf{A}=\xpos )
    \,,\label{eq:causal,cff}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for all $y$ and for any value $\xpos'$ attainable by $\mathsf{A}$. 


CFF is individual-level and agnostic to the accuracy of the predictor $f(\cdot)$. It substantially differs from comparing individuals who happen to share the same `treatment' $\mathsf{A}=\xpos$ and coincide on identical values of $\mathsf{X}$, as differences between $\mathsf{X}_{\mathsf{A}\gets \xpos}$ and $\mathsf{X}_{\mathsf{A}\gets \xpos'}$ must be solely caused by variations in $\mathsf{A}$. 

In addition, CFF assumes that any causal effect of $\mathsf{A}$ (a sensitive attribute) on predictions is deemed illegitimate. A broader definition \citep{nabi2018fair,boeschoten2021achieving}, where CFF is a special case, distinguishes between discriminatory and non-discriminatory causal pathways from $\mathsf{A}$. Instead of enforcing  $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{do}(\xpos)}(f|\mathsf{X})= \mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{do}(\xpos')}(f|\mathsf{X})$ as in CFF, fairness is achieved by inferring a distribution $\mathbb{P}^*(\mathsf{y|X})$ that closely approximates $\mathbb{P}(\mathsf{y|X})$ (in a Kullback-Leibler sense) while blocking discriminatory pathways within a given tolerance using causal inference techniques. 


\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proxy discrimination \citep{kilbertus2017avoiding}]
\label{def:causal,pd}
Given a generic causal graph structure (a DAG), 
involving a protected attribute $\mathsf{A}_1$, a set of proxy variables $\mathsf{P}$, features $\mathsf{X}$, a predictor $f(\cdot)$, and sometimes an observed outcome $\mathsf{y}$. 
The predictor $f(\cdot)$ exhibits no \emph{individual proxy discrimination}, if for all $\xneg$ and all $\mathbf{p},\mathbf{p}'$,
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \mathbb{P}(f\!\mid \mathrm{do}(\mathsf{P}\!=\!\mathbf{p}), \mathsf{X}\!=\!\xneg)=
    \mathbb{P}(f\!\mid \mathrm{do}(\mathsf{P}\!=\!\mathbf{p}'), \mathsf{X}\!=\!\xneg)\,.
\end{equation}
The predictor $f(\cdot)$ exhibits no \emph{proxy discrimination in expectation}, if for all $\mathbf{p},\mathbf{p}'$,
\begin{equation}
    \topequation\small
    \mathbb{E}[f\!\mid \mathrm{do}(\mathsf{P}= \mathbf{p})]=
    \mathbb{E}[f\!\mid \mathrm{do}(\mathsf{P}= \mathbf{p}')]\,.
\end{equation}
The proxy $\mathsf{P}$ and the features $\mathsf{X}$ could be multidimensional. 
\end{definition}






