\paragraph{Necessity of considering multi-valued scenarios}
Most existing fairness measures, as summerised in Table~\ref{tab:fair,summary}, are designed to work with a single SA, typically binary in nature. 
These include commonly used group fairness measures (such as demographic parity, equality of opportunity, and predictive parity) and the Lipschitz condition. 
However, real-world scenarios often involve multiple values---such as race, age, religion, gender, or sexual orientation---depending on the data collected. As such, these existing measures may fail to accurately estimate the real level of discrimination in ML systems. 
If discrimination is underestimated, it can lead to a ``discriminative Matthew effect'', where ML algorithms amplify the human biases embedded in the data they are trained on \citep{glickman2024human}. 

While the original definitions of these fairness measures could be extended to handle multi-valued SAs (as discussed in \eqref{eq:grp,extend} and \eqref{eq:grp,extend,alter}), differences still exist among these extensions. 
Taking DP as an example, \eqref{eq:grp,ext,b} provides a more fine-grained approach than \eqref{eq:grp,ext,a}, but it comes at a higher computational cost, making it more time-consuming. 
Thus, it would be advantageous to have a fairness measure that can efficiently address discrimination in such cases. 
The extensions in \eqref{eq:grp,extend,alter} are more generic, as they do not assume the number of privileged groups in advance, nor do they require prior knowledge of which group(s) are privileged. 
Yet computing them can be pretty costly, compared to \eqref{eq:grp,extend}.\looseness=-1





\paragraph{Necessity of considering scenarios with more than one sensitive attribute}
Some researchers may suggest simplifying the scenario with multiple SAs by transforming them into a single SA through pre-processing. 
For example, consider data with two SAs $\mathcal{A}\!= \mathcal{A}_1\!\times\!\mathcal{A}_2 \!=\! \mathbb{Z}^{n_{a_1}}\!\times\!\mathbb{Z}^{n_{a_2}}$, where $n_{a_1}, n_{a_2} \!\geqslant\! 2$. 
After pre-processing, the combined single SA would be $\mathcal{A}'= \mathbb{Z}^{n_{a_1}\!\times n_{a_2}}$. 
This approach may be practical when both $n_{a_1}$ and $n_{a_2}$ are small enough, yet the computational cost increases exponentially as these values grow 
(\eg{} if $n_{a_1}\!=\!2$ and $n_{a_2}$ changes from $2$ to $6$, $\mathcal{A}'$ transitions from $\mathbb{Z}^4$ to $\mathbb{Z}^{12}$). Similarly, if the number of SAs itself increases, the computational burden also rises (\eg{} $\mathcal{A}'$ could shift from $\mathbb{Z}^{2\times 6} \!=\! \mathbb{Z}^{12}$ to $\mathbb{Z}^{2\times 6\times 3} \!=\! \mathbb{Z}^{36}$). 
The larger the number of values for each SA, the longer it takes to compute. 
In such cases, the computational cost may be reduced if one fairness measure can handle multiple SAs directly. This approach can be viewed as decomposing the original complex problem into smaller and more manageable sub-problems, akin to the divide-and-conquer strategy.\looseness=-1 



