\paragraph{Empirical results} 
We present some preliminary results to demonstrate our first position: why existing fairness measures are not sufficient enough for state-of-the-art fairness research in ML. 
These experiments are conducted to explore the following research questions: 
\textbf{RQ1}. Why do we need to handle cases of one multi-valued SA specifically, rather than treating them as cases of one bi-valued SA simply?
\textbf{RQ2}. Why do we need to address multiple-SAs cases specifically, rather than handling them as one multi-valued SA after preprocessing? 
The empirical results are presented in Figures~\ref{fig:stats,criteria} and \ref{fig:tim,criteria}, as well as Figures~\ref{fig:criteria1}, \ref{fig:criteria2}, and \ref{fig:tim,cont}. 
%
We use five public datasets, that is, Ricci,\footnote{\url{https://rdrr.io/cran/Stat2Data/man/Ricci.html}} 
Credit,\footnote{\url{https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/statlog+(german+credit+data}} 
Income,\footnote{\url{https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/adult}} 
PPR, and PPVR;\footnote{\url{https://github.com/propublica/compas-analysis/}, that is,\\Propublica-Recidivism (PPR) and Propublica-Violent-Recidivism (PPVR) datasets} 
Each of them has two sensitive attributes except the Ricci dataset. 
We mainly use bagging, AdaBoost, LightGBM \citep{ke2017lightgbm}, FairGBM \citep{cruz2022fairgbm}, and AdaFair \citep{iosifidis2019adafair} as learning algorithms, where FairGBM and AdaFair are two fairness-aware ensemble-based methods. 
Standard 5-fold cross-validation is used in these experiments, in other words, in each iteration, the entire dataset is divided into two parts, with 80\% as the training set and 20\% as the test set. 
Also, features of datasets are scaled in preprocessing to lie between 0 and 1. 

As we can see from Figure~\ref{fig:stats,criteria}, except for the first modification in Eq.~\eqref{eq:grp,ext,a}, all other three modifications---\ie{} Eq. \eqref{eq:grp,ext,b}, \eqref{eq:grp,alt,a}, and \eqref{eq:grp,alt,b}---correlate differently with the performance change, that is, $\Delta$(performance). 
It means that we should be careful when choosing the modified version of these fairness measures, as they may result in different observations; It also means that when dealing with one multi-valued SA cases, treating it as one bi-valued SA case is not the only way and may not be the best way to handle the problem. 
Similar observations can also be found in Figures~\ref{fig:criteria1} and \ref{fig:criteria2}.\looseness=-1 
%
As for the time cost it requires, handling cases of one multi-valued SA usually costs more time than treating it as the case of one bi-valued SA, as shown in Figures~\ref{fig:tim,criteria} and \ref{fig:tim,cont}. 
It means that the more values one SA has, the more time it requires when computing fairness measures. 
Therefore, it can be inferred that simply pre-processing multiple SAs into one may not be the best solution to address the problem, supporting our explanation in the previous subsubsection. 



To sum up, simply handling multi-valued SAs like bi-valued cases may cause some unknown information loss, as different modifications come with various correlations compared with $\Delta(\text{performance})$; 
handling multi-valued SAs by pairwise methods may increase the time cost, indicating the necessity of seeking more efficient ways to take care of such cases. 
Therefore, existing fairness measures may be limited in the face of one multi-valued SA and even more SAs, which is why we need more designed fairness measures even though we already have many in the literature.






\subsection{Can fairness be discussed solely from a mathematical or computational perspective?}





This question touches on various perspectives observed in discussions with researchers, including:
\begin{itemize}
\setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
\item \textbf{Fairness beyond protected attributes}: Some argue that \emph{fairness is supposed to apply to any attributes, not just those traditionally considered as protected attributes (\eg{} race, gender, disability, age, and sexual orientation).} 
%
For instance, one person may be discriminated due to their eye colour or hair colour. Another example is that discrimination may occur against people with different food habits simply as it can be done. 
\item \textbf{Unknown privileged group in protected attributes}: \emph{Even for a given protected attribute, the privileged group might not be clearly identified, and it is unlikely to be just one group, so ~\eqref{eq:grp,extend} is not correct because they are not symmetrical.} 
%
For example, in the case of ``Median Income by Race in the United States'', Asian individuals earn higher salaries than white individuals, so Asians should be considered as the privileged group in this case instead of white people. This also suggests the possibility of two privileged groups if the white is considered as privileged as well.  
\item \textbf{Mathematical vs. social perspective}: Another view is that \emph{it is not the job of computer scientists to decide who is privileged or disadvantaged, because they lack the expertise in social sciences}. Computer scientists, according to this opinion, should only define fairness mathematically, and these definitions should apply universally across all contexts (otherwise they are wrong). Thus, if the fairness measure is well-defined mathematically, there is little room for further debate. 
\end{itemize}




We do not agree with these viewpoints. 

Before delving into the contradictions of these arguments, we would first pose a couple of broad questions: 
Should scientists care about ethical issues when developing new technologies, or is the goal simply to make technology as advanced as possible? 
Shall scientists consider universal values when developing advanced technologies? 
%
Our view is that technology itself is neutral, but how technology is used can result in positive or negative consequences. 
Therefore, it is critical not only to develop technology but also to use it carefully and wisely. 
While mathematics and algorithms are central to technological progress, we argue that scientists, especially those working in fields like machine learning, should learn from the social sciences as well. Technology impacts society in profound ways, and an understanding of societal contexts, ethical implications, and fairness is crucial to ensuring that the benefits of new technologies are distributed justly and that potential harms are mitigated. 
Simply and icy saying, ``I care only about mathematics,'' or passing responsibility to another department (\eg{} the Department of Humanities), is not sufficient.\footnote{
%
Solely considering fairness mathematically  
may result in misalignment with real-world needs, inappropriate application for focusing more on correlations over causation, and seemingly-fair-but-actually-biassed solutions due to ignoring deeper systemic issues that create disparities in the first place. 
%
} Ethical considerations should be an integral part of technological development. In this sense, fairness is not just a technical challenge but a societal one, and a well-rounded understanding of both fields---technical and social---is essential for researchers.\looseness=-1 









In addressing the alternative views, we begin by clearly stating our disagreement with the \emph{third viewpoint}. The argument that ``not having some knowledge is a good reason to refuse to engage with something'' is fundamentally flawed. If it is the right thing to do, we believe that acquiring the relevant knowledge is essential. 
Would we, for example, accept a researcher's claim that they do not conduct experiments or ablation studies because they ``do not know how to'', and therefore expect their manuscript to be accepted without further scrutiny? If this reasoning is not acceptable in the context of empirical work, why should it be different when it comes to interdisciplinary knowledge or topics like fairness in ML? 
While it may not be feasible for a single individual to possess expertise across all fields, it is critical to resolve problems to the best of our ability rather than facilely ignore them, especially when they are clearly relevant. In fairness, the issue arises due to societal concerns like race and gender discrimination, which have real-world implications. How can we claim to solve the problem of fairness without considering societal scenarios or forms of discrimination?\looseness=-1 

Even if we proceed without considering societal contexts, would the problem we are ``solving'' still address the same underlying issue? This ties into the \emph{first alternative view}---whether fairness should apply to all attributes. We believe there is a distinction between ``getting different results by choices or by unprotected attributes'' and ``discriminating against someone''. The former does not necessarily involve treating someone differently in a harmful or unfair manner. 
For instance, consider a situation where employees at a company are served different types of food based on their preferences (\eg{} vegetarian versus meat). Would this scenario be considered discrimination against vegetarians? Is this analogous to race or gender discrimination? Similarly, if assistant professors receive lower salaries than full professors, does that constitute discrimination against assistant professors? Or is it discrimination that people in different professions (like banking and security) receive different salaries? We argue that those who frame these examples as discrimination are confusing different concepts and deliberately neglecting the marginalised groups who truly suffer. The example of eye or hair colour is often tied to race or ethnicity and thus serves as a proxy attribute for protected charateristics, rather than a truly unprotected attribute. Attributes including education or wealth are often proxies for protected characteristics such as gender, race, or ethnicity. It is essential to be cautious when dealing with such proxy attributes, as they can indirectly reflect discrimination tied to protected attributes.\looseness=-1 



Turning to the \emph{second alternative view}, the observation that salary disparities exist among groups (\eg{} Asians earning higher salaries than whites in the US) does not imply that race is the direct cause of the salary difference. Consider the example of foreign workers in Denmark, where salary requirements are tied to work-permit eligibility. If foreign workers earn higher salaries than local Danish citizens due to these requirements, does this mean that foreign workers are the ``privileged group''? It is vital to distinguish between correlation and causality. Assuming that salaries are solely driven by race would be misleading and may lead to untrue conclusions, such as reverse causality. Discrimination cannot be fully understood through mathematics alone; social and historical contexts must be considered. 

Regarding the \emph{question of privileged groups in multi-valued SAs}, while it is true that the number of privileged groups may depend on the specific context, studying the case where there is only one privileged group can still yield valuable insights. For example, with the race attribute, one racial group may be considered primarily privileged relative to others. Although other groups within the marginalised category may still have internal hierarchies of privilege, focusing on the most privileged group allows us to simplify the problem and study it from a starting point. Once we understand the dynamics of a single privileged group, we can extend the analysis to more complex scenarios with multiple privileged groups.\looseness=-1 
















\section{Conclusion and Outlook}
\label{sec:concl}


To sum up, we argue that existing fairness measures are limited in scope and insufficient for addressing the complexities of real-world problems. 
Most current measures are designed for one bi-valued SA, yet many real-life scenarios involve multiple SAs with multi-valued categories. To ensure fairness in ML systems, it is essential to develop fairness measures that can effectively handle these more complex cases.\looseness=-1 

Moreover, we emphasise the necessity of integrating social considerations into fairness research in ML. Fairness is not merely a mathematical abstraction---it is inherently tied to societal values and real-world implications. Addressing fairness issues solely through technical methods, without acknowledging their social context, risks oversimplifying the problem and potentially reinforcing existing biases.


Building a better future requires more than just neutral algorithms and advanced technology. Rational technological thinking and humanistic considerations should not be viewed as opposing forces but rather as complementary perspectives. By integrating both, particularly in socially relevant domains like fairness in ML, we can work towards a more just and equitable society---not just for ourselves, but for future generations. 












