\subsubsection{Deep Implicit Representations for Automotive Shapes}
Below we outline our approach using DeepSDF to encode automotive shapes into continuous implicit representations, enabling efficient shape retrieval and generative modeling. DeepSDF~\cite{park2019deepsdf} is a neural implicit representation technique that encodes 3D shapes into a continuous Signed Distance Function (SDF) space, allowing for efficient shape reconstruction and interpolation. Our approach extends DeepSDF for car design by encoding car meshes into a latent space representation while introducing modifications to improve training efficiency and reconstruction quality. Recent research has leveraged DeepSDF for 3D car generation and customization. Morita et al.~\cite{morita2024vehiclesdf} introduced VehicleSDF, enabling the generation of diverse 3D car models while estimating aerodynamic performance via surrogate modeling. Similarly, Miao et al.~\cite{miao2024manipulating} proposed a framework for precise, attribute-specific modifications to both stylistic and geometric aspects of car designs, ensuring structural integrity.


\paragraph{DeepSDF Training Data, Network Architecture and Modifications}

To train our CAD Agent, we require a dataset that includes SDF samples which are essential for training implicit neural shape representations. To generate the training data, we normalize each car mesh to fit within a unit sphere and sample 500,000 spatial points, with a higher concentration near the surface. Each point $(x, y, z)$ is associated with an SDF value that represents the signed distance to the nearest surface.

DeepSDF uses an auto-decoder framework, where a latent vector $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ is learned for each training shape. The network consists of a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with eight fully connected layers of 512 neurons each, utilizing ReLU activations. Given an input coordinate $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{3}$ and the latent vector representation $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$, the model predicts the corresponding SDF value $f(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z})$.





To improve training efficiency, accuracy, and performance specific to car designs, we introduce the following modifications:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Positional Encoding:} Inspired by recent advancements in neural implicit representations~\cite{miao2024manipulating}, we apply a Fourier feature mapping to input coordinates:
    \begin{equation}
        \gamma(\mathbf{x}) = \left( \sin(2^k \pi \mathbf{x}), \cos(2^k \pi \mathbf{x}) \right), \quad k=0, \dots, 8
    \end{equation}
    This transformation aids in learning high-frequency geometric details.
    \item \textbf{Reduced Latent Space Dimension:} The latent vector dimension is reduced from 256 to 16 to optimize storage and improve generalization in the constrained design space of automotive shapes.
    % \item \textbf{Regularization on Latent Codes:} Rather than using L2 norm regularization, we apply L2 regularization on individidual elements in the latent codes to encourage a more dense latent representation.
\end{itemize}


\paragraph{Training Procedure and Reconstruction}

The model is trained on SDF samples using L1 loss on predicted SDF values, with L2 regularization applied to the latent codes. During training, both the model parameters and latent codes are optimized simultaneously using the Adam optimizer. The loss function is defined as

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}), s) = \left| \text{clamp}(f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}), \pm\delta) - \text{clamp}(s, \pm\delta) \right|
\end{equation}

where $\text{clamp}(x, \delta) := \min(\delta, \max(-\delta, x))$ ensures that the model focuses its representational ability near the surface. 
The total loss for optimizing the model and latent codes is:

\begin{equation}
    \arg \min_{\theta, \{z_i\}_{i=1}^{N}} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left( \sum_{j=1}^{K} \mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(z_i, \gamma(\mathbf{x}_j)), s_j) + \lambda \|z_i\|_2^2 \right)
\end{equation}


Given a target shape, we find the associated latent code by using the Adam optimizer to minimize the reconstruction loss. Then, we query the DeepSDF model at spatial points $(x, y, z)$ and extract the final mesh using the Marching Cubes algorithm~\cite{lorensen1998marching}.
% Given a query shape, we optimize the latent vector $\mathbf{z}$ using the Adam optimizer to minimize the reconstruction loss. The final mesh is extracted using the Marching Cubes algorithm~\cite{lorensen1998marching}. 
To explore the learned latent space, we perform interpolation between latent representations of different car designs, enabling smooth transitions between shape variations.

\subsubsection{Retrieval of 3D Car Models from 2D Sketches}
Here we demonstrate how the CAD Agent retrieves the most similar 3D car geometries from DrivAerNet++ based on input sketches.



\paragraph{Latent-Code-Based Shape Retrieval}
This subsection describes how latent space representations (from DeepSDF) enable efficient identification of matching car geometries based on sketch inputs.
Our approach involves training a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to predict the latent code associated with each car mesh based on the input sketch. The CNN takes a sketch as input and outputs the predicted DeepSDF latent code. The model is trained to minimize the L2 loss between the predicted latent code and the ground truth latent code.
We use a single CNN model for all car classes. During retrieval, the input consists of a car class and a sketch, and the output is the car mesh with the closest matching latent code, determined by minimizing the L2 distance between the predicted latent code and the latent codes of meshes within the specified car class:


\begin{equation}
    \hat{z} = f_{\theta}(\text{sketch}, c)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \text{mesh} = \arg\min_{\text{mesh} \in \mathcal{M}_c} \left\| z_{\text{mesh}} - \hat{z} \right\|_2
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \( f_{\theta} \) is a CNN trained to predict the DeepSDF latent code from a given sketch.
    \item \( c \) is the specified car class.
    \item \( \hat{z} \) is the predicted latent code of the input sketch.
    \item \( z_{\text{mesh}} \) represents the latent codes of available 3D car meshes.
    \item \( \mathcal{M}_c \) is the set of all meshes belonging to the car class \( c \).
    \item The retrieval process selects the mesh whose DeepSDF latent code minimizes the L2 distance to the predicted latent code \( \hat{z} \).
\end{itemize}


