\subsection{Result}

The results clearly demonstrate that BemaGANv2 achieves superior performance in both short- and long-term audio tasks, outperforming all other models in objective and subjective evaluations. This highlights the effectiveness of its generator design and discriminator configuration, particularly in preserving both high-frequency details and long-term periodic structures.

Although BemaGANv2 shows relatively low SSIM scores, this can be attributed to its behavior in reconstructing amplitude levels. SSIM is computed by comparing Mel-spectrogram images across three RGB channels, where the color intensity primarily reflects the sound loudness. The lower SSIM indicates that BemaGANv2 tends to deviate from the exact decibel scale of the original audio. However, subjective metrics such as MOS and SMOS consistently rank BemaGANv2 as the top performer. This suggests that human listeners are more sensitive to the perceptual fidelity of the audio, such as timbral and structural quality, than to precise restoration of loudness. In support of this, BemaGANv2 also achieves the best scores in FAD and MCD, which are directly related to perceptual realism and spectral coherence.

Interestingly, although the MED+MPD+MRD combination yielded decent objective metrics — especially in long-term evaluation — it showed significantly lower subjective scores. Through an analysis of training and validation loss curves, we confirmed that the issue is not due to overfitting. Instead, we attribute this degradation in perceptual quality to mode collapse\cite{b17}, likely caused by adversarial instability when employing an excessive number of discriminators. Following this observation, we did not pursue further experiments involving combinations of three or more discriminators, as the risk of mode collapse outweighed potential performance gains.

These findings emphasize the importance of balancing feedback diversity and adversarial stability in discriminator design. For further experimental details, including loss curves and configuration analysis, please refer to ~\ref{B}.


\section{Discussion}

During the evaluation of vocoder outputs, we observed a peculiar issue in the HiFi-GAN model where long-term audio samples resulted in outputs nearly double the expected length. This phenomenon was not observed in other models (e.g., BigVGAN, BemaGANv2), despite identical training data, architectures, and preprocessing pipelines.

To identify the source of this anomaly, we systematically investigated several components of the inference pipeline:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Configuration consistency between training and inference (e.g., \texttt{hop\_size}, \texttt{n\_fft}) — confirmed to be consistent.
    \item STFT and Mel-spectrogram center alignment — identical across models and not responsible for the anomaly.
    \item Dataset bias — all models were trained on LJSpeech, which consists of short, speech-based audio samples.
    \item Model architecture — no differences except the activation function.
\end{itemize}

The only significant difference between HiFi-GAN and the other models lies in the activation function: HiFi-GAN utilizes LeakyReLU\cite{b2}, while others incorporate the Snake function. Given that Snake is known to better extrapolate periodic structures, especially in long audio sequences\cite{b5}, we conclude that the root cause of the abnormal doubling in waveform duration stems from HiFi-GAN's inability to generalize beyond the training distribution of short-length signals.

This result underscores the critical role of periodic inductive bias in vocoders tasked with long-term synthesis. Snake-based models consistently exhibit better stability and fidelity.

For reproducibility and further inspection, debugging logs and comparative duration graphs are provided in the ~\ref{C}

\section{Conclusion}

In this study, we presented BemaGANv2, a vocoder designed to enhance both the fidelity and temporal coherence of waveform reconstruction. Through theoretical motivations and extensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrated that BemaGANv2 outperforms existing GAN-based vocoders such as HiFi-GAN and BigVGAN, particularly in long-duration audio tasks. The model's generator architecture incorporating AMP blocks and the proposed Multi-Envelope Discriminator (MED) proved effective in capturing both local details and long-term periodicity.

Our results showed that BemaGANv2 achieves superior performance in both objective and subjective evaluation metrics in short- and long-term audio. Notably, it exhibited perceptual advantages even in scenarios where traditional metrics such as SSIM were lower, highlighting its ability to preserve the auditory quality that human listeners prioritize.

These findings collectively suggest that BemaGANv2 is a highly suitable vocoder for Text-to-Music (TTM) and Text-to-Audio (TTA) models, especially diffusion based generation pipelines. As a next step, we plan to integrate BemaGANv2 into multimodal generative AI systems, enabling more expressive and temporally coherent audio generation conditioned on various modalities. We believe this direction will further bridge the gap between machine-generated and human-perceived audio realism.

